  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front will pass through the region this \nafternoon and evening.\n\nA cold front will approach later today and move across the \nregion this afternoon and evening. Surface dew points will rise \ninto the 70s today, and PWATs will be in excess of 2 inches. \nSBCAPE values will increase to 1000 to 1500 J/kg as well with \nhigh temperatures in the mid to upper 80s. Although there is the\npotential for some brief, locally heavy rain, shear will be \nlimited and organized convection is not likely. PoPs will be \ncapped at chance.\n\nBehind the cold front, high temperatures on Tuesday will be\nsimilar to Monday, but dew points will drop from the low to mid\n70s to the mid 60s. While warm, it should feel more comfortable\non Tuesday compared to Monday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front is expected to cross through the \nregion Wednesday night into Thursday resulting in more shower \nand thunderstorm chances.\n\nIn the wake of tonight's cold front, weak high pressure will \nbriefly build into the region for Tuesday and much of Wednesday.\nAn upper-low will traverse portions of Ontario and Quebec \nduring this time, passing north of the region Wednesday night. \nTo the south of the upper-low, broad troughing will be in place \nacross much of the Northeast, with a few embedded shortwaves \npivoting around the base of the trough. At the surface, a cold \nfront will begin to approach the area late Wednesday, and cross \nthrough the region Wednesday night into Thursday. \n\nTiming discrepancies remain with the frontal passage amongst \nmodel guidance. Some guidance indicates the front passing \nthrough the region primarily during the overnight hours \nWednesday night and clearing the region Thursday morning, while \nthere is still some guidance to suggest slower timing. At this \npoint, timing uncertainties limit confidence in whether or not \nthere will be any notable severe weather or flash flooding \nconcerns, but this will continue to bear watching, particularly \nif the slower solutions come to fruition. For now, PoPs peak in \nthe 40-60% range across most of the area on Thursday. \n\nThere are also some indications that the pattern may remain \nunsettled into the weekend as the front could get hung up just \nsouth of the region with a couple of areas of low pressure \nriding along it and mid-level troughing remaining in place. PoPs\nare currently in the 20-40% range each day due to the present \nuncertainties in timing and placement of these features. \n\nIn terms of temps, Tuesday and Wednesday look to be fairly warm\nwith modestly increasing humidity. Highs look to be in the \nupper 80s to lower 90s. Temps currently look a bit cooler in \nthe wake of the front, with highs in the low to mid 80s and lows\nin the upper 50s to mid 60s late this week and into the \nweekend.",
